<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kathmandu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kathmandu is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> in the Himalayan foothills. As Kantipur it was the seat of one of the three Malla kingdoms of the valley, whose rulers built much of its Durbar Square. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of Gorkha captured it in 1768 and made it the capital of the unified Kingdom of Nepal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kathmandu.jpg" alt="Kathmandu" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerd Eichmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathmandu-Durbar_Square-06-Mahavishnu-Kuh-Vishnu-Pratapamalla-Jagannath-2007-gje.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kathmandu lies in the northwestern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, north of the Bagmati River, at an elevation of about 1,324 metres. The city covers 50.7 square kilometres and is bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a>, also called Patan, with which it forms one urban area surrounded by a ring road. It is dissected by eight rivers, of which the main one is the Bagmati. The valley is an ancient lake basin with fertile soil and flat terrain, which helped form a society based on agriculture.</p><p>Its position between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> made it an important trading centre. Trade flourished along an offshoot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> linking India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, and Lhasa Newar merchants of Kathmandu conducted trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> and helped spread art styles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological excavations in Kathmandu have found evidence of ancient civilisations. The oldest finding is a statue from Maligaon dated to 185 CE. The excavation of Dhando Chaitya uncovered a brick with an inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a>, which archaeologists believe is two thousand years old. Historically the valley was known as the Nepal Mandala and was the cultural and political hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar people</a>. From ancient times until the medieval period Kathmandu was known as Kantipur, which derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words meaning beauty and place, giving the sense of city of light.</p><p>The name Kathmandu comes from Kasthamandap, a public pavilion made of wood, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words for wood and pavilion. It was rebuilt in 1596 in the period of King Lakshmi Narasimha Malla, and according to legend all its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> came from a single tree. The earliest Western reference to the city is in the account of the Portuguese Jesuit Father Joao Cabral, who passed through the valley in the spring of 1628 and was received by the king, probably Lakshminarasimha Malla. He reported that they reached Cadmendu, the capital of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kathmandu is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and its main governmental, financial, industrial and cultural centre. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded a population of 845,767, with approximately 4 million in the surrounding metropolitan area. Its people are multi-ethnic, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> majority and a significant Vajrayana Buddhist presence. Since 1985 it has hosted the headquarters of the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist practice has influenced the monuments of the city over the centuries. In 2006 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared seven groups of monuments in the valley a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, among them Durbar Square, Swayambhu Mahachaitya, Bouddha and Pashupatinath.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kathmandu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Narayan Shah, who became king of Gorkha in 1743, set out to bring under his rule the many principalities of the central Himalaya, among them the western confederations known as the Chaubisi and Baisi kingdoms and the three Newar kingdoms of the valley: <b>Kathmandu</b>, Lalitpur (Patan) and Bhaktapur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bon’s leading monastery in India is the refounded Menri at Dolanji in Himachal Pradesh, and in Nepal Triten Norbutse stands on the western outskirts of <b>Kathmandu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake of an estimated magnitude between 8.2 and 8.8 struck northern Nepal, also affecting southern China and northern India and causing severe damage in <b>Kathmandu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-6">6 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1768 Prithvi Narayan Shah captured <b>Kathmandu</b>, followed by Patan and Bhaktapur, and moved his capital there, creating the Kingdom of Nepal.</blockquote><a href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom controlled routes to Tibet in the east and competed with <b>Kathmandu</b> and Patan for trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bhaktapur">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</a></li><li><blockquote>Kantipur emerged in 1484 when Ratna Malla, a son of Yaksha Malla of the Malla dynasty, established himself at <b>Kathmandu</b> after his father's death.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kantipur">Kingdom of Kantipur</a></li></ul></section>
